/T (Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing \(AST\) Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing \(AST\) is a crucial procedure in clinical microbiology used to determine the susceptibility or resistance of pathogenic bacteria to various antimicrobial agents. The results of AST guide clinicians in selecting the most effective antibiotic for treating infections, thereby improving patient outcomes and reducing the misuse of antibiotics. In hospital laboratories, AST plays a vital role in controlling the spread of antimicrobial resistance, which has emerged as a major global health concern.)
/E (Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing \(AST\) Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing \(AST\) is a crucial procedure in clinical microbiology used to determine the susceptibility or resistance of pathogenic bacteria to various antimicrobial agents. The results of AST guide clinicians in selecting the most effective antibiotic for treating infections, thereby improving patient outcomes and reducing the misuse of antibiotics. In hospital laboratories, AST plays a vital role in controlling the spread of antimicrobial resistance, which has emerged as a major global health concern.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Principle of Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing The principle of antibiotic sensitivity testing is based on the ability of an antibiotic to inhibit the growth of a microorganism. When a bacterial culture is exposed to an antibiotic, susceptible bacteria fail to grow near the antibiotic source, while resistant bacteria continue to grow. This inhibitory effect is visualized as a clear zone around the antibiotic, known as the zone of inhibition. The size of this zone is directly related to the sensitivity of the organism to the antibiotic.)
/E (Principle of Antibiotic Sensitivity Testing The principle of antibiotic sensitivity testing is based on the ability of an antibiotic to inhibit the growth of a microorganism. When a bacterial culture is exposed to an antibiotic, susceptible bacteria fail to grow near the antibiotic source, while resistant bacteria continue to grow. This inhibitory effect is visualized as a clear zone around the antibiotic, known as the zone of inhibition. The size of this zone is directly related to the sensitivity of the organism to the antibiotic.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
